WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:10.520
Time is a dimension.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:15.720
Just like length, breadth or height.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:18.880
But whereas we can move
in any direction

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:20.880
in those other three dimensions

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.840
we can only move
in one direction of time -

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:26.640
forward, relentlessly.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:28.480
Why?

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.400
Why can't we ever move
backwards in time?

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:35.520
For a long while
scientists couldn't come up

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.240
with a convincing explanation.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:38.440
The laws of physics, you see,

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:41.840
work fine whether you're going
forwards or backwards in time.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:46.280
Their answer finally came
from an unexpected place -

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.040
steam engines.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.200
At the start
of the industrial revolution

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.080
engineers were trying to understand

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.720
how to make steam engines
more efficient.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:00.440
In examining how all that heat
and energy moved around an engine,

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.000
they developed a whole new
branch of science

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:06.640
which they called, appropriately,
thermodynamics.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.320
But it turned out that thermodynamics

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:13.720
could explain the behaviour of a lot
more than just steam engines.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.440
In particular, the second law of
thermodynamics helps us to understand

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.920
why things happen
in the order that they do.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.160
A cup smashes on the floor
for example,

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:28.880
and spills out all its contents.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.360
Now, we inherently know that
that process is irreversible.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:35.080
Cups smash.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:38.600
Things have a way of
disorganising themselves

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:41.960
but they're not so good
at reorganising themselves

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:45.400
and the second law of thermodynamics
tells us why.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.360
Another way of looking at it
is in terms of messiness.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:53.560
A cup is tidy.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.720
A smashed cup is messy.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.040
The word for this in physics is...

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.520
The more entropy there is in a place,

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:05.720
the more disordered,
messy and useless it is.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.400
Here's what the second law
of thermodynamics looks like.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:11.880
This 'S' represents entropy

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:17.200
and this 'd' is a mathematical way
of representing change.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.280
So 'dS' simply means
a change in entropy.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.960
Now if you look at this equation
from left to right

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.000
what it says is that
the entropy of a system

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:28.840
always has to increase.

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:33.000
When a cup smashes
or a freezer defrosts

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:36.400
that's fine according to
the second law of thermodynamics

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.280
because the entropy
of those things increases.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.440
But if you expect melted ice
to reassemble

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.520
you're expecting entropy to fall.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:52.120
That would break the second law.
That's a no-no.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:00.640
The second law tells us what order
things can happen in the universe.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.000
It gives us a clear direction
for the flow of what we call time -

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:06.280
forward.

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:09.120
Time simply can't flow any other way

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.840
because that would decrease entropy
and break the second law.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:17.000
But where is the relentless march
of time taking us?

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:22.520
The entropy of the universe,
the disorder is always increasing.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.120
Always.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:28.920
At some point in the far future

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:34.200
this means that our entire universe
will be in a state of total disorder,

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:36.760
a state of maximum entropy.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:39.280
Scientists call it the 'heat death'.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:43.280
This is how our universe will end.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:48.200
But don't worry, this bleak fate
won't come upon us any time soon.

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:50.480
The universe
will have to wait trillions

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.040
and trillions
and trillions more years,

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.360
by which time of course
there will be no humans left to see

00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:00.120
how time and entropy
have ravaged our universe.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:10.080
Thanks for watching. 
